WEBVTT

00:00:00.414 --> 00:00:04.435
Quantization lowers the numerical precision used for model weights and sometimes other

00:00:04.435 --> 00:00:05.000
data.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.145
It reduces memory and bandwidth pressure,

00:00:07.145 --> 00:00:11.000
making local inference practical on hardware that cannot support the original precision.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:15.000
The savings can improve model loading and allow more layers to remain on an accelerator,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
but scales, metadata, activations, runtime buffers,

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.240
and context cache still consume memory.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.016
Precision labels describe tradeoffs, not guaranteed outcomes.

00:00:23.016 --> 00:00:27.695
FP16, BF16, INT8, and low-bit formats require compatible hardware and runtimes.

00:00:27.695 --> 00:00:31.195
GGUF and safetensors are containers used in different workflows;

00:00:31.195 --> 00:00:34.000
neither extension alone establishes quality or precision.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.960
Tokenizers, templates, architecture metadata, and model revisions must remain compatible.

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.005
Q4 is often a useful baseline,

00:00:40.005 --> 00:00:43.000
while lower levels favor fit and higher levels favor fidelity.

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:48.440
The right choice depends on model architecture, available VRAM or RAM, context, batch,

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:50.985
offload, kernel support, and task sensitivity.

00:00:50.985 --> 00:00:54.340
A model that barely fits may run worse than a smaller or more compact option with

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:54.960
headroom.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.046
Choose through testing.

00:00:56.046 --> 00:00:59.150
Compare several variants with the same runtime, prompts, settings, and hardware.

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:02.960
Measure speed and memory, evaluate factual and task quality, repeat trials,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.000
and record the configuration.

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.210
Quantization succeeds when it preserves the quality required by the workload while

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.000
delivering stable,

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.000
maintainable performance on the target system.
